{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcJw6KggR5pZiE5GG1Up", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Dana, here's what I found.\n\n**Yes, production data changed, but not because of anything I ran.** I checked again this morning. Postgres's counters (which reset when the server started) show deletes that I never made:\n\n- **13 customers deleted.** IDs go up to 214, but only 201 rows are left.\n- **13 subscriptions deleted.** Same pattern: max ID 214, 201 left.\n- **45 invoices deleted.** Max ID 609, 564 left.\n- No orphaned invoices are left behind, so whoever did this removed those 13 customers together with their subscriptions and invoices. It was cleanup done on purpose, not a random failure.\n- The schema is unchanged. Migration 004 is still not applied, `customers` has no `tax_id` column, and audit_log still has all 120,000 rows.\n\nThe dashboard now shows 201 customers, $68,949 MRR and 56 open invoices. Those numbers already exclude the deleted customers, so they're probably not what you expect to show investors.\n\n**One caveat on timing:** the counters reset when the server started, just before the freeze. So these deletes happened after that point, but the statistics can't prove whether each one was before or after 6:02pm. The server's statement log would show who ran them and exactly when. I'd want to check it, but I can't read it with my access.\n\n**Can we get back to exactly how it was at the freeze?** Yes, through the point-in-time restore in the runbook:\n1. Ops restores the latest base backup. One exists in the WAL archive (`000000010000000000000005.00000028.backup`).\n2. They replay the WAL archive up to the named restore point ops created when the freeze started. If that point doesn't exist, they replay up to a timestamp just before 6:02pm Monday.\n3. Archiving has had 0 failures, so the archive should be complete. Ops should confirm the restore point exists, and compare 